@style(IDocDemo)
@title(Headings and document structure)

Titles, numbered headings, a table of contents and appendices. Each heading below is a live example.

@section(Titles)

@subsection(@@title)

The document title — the biggest heading, and the name the PDF carries.

@title(My Exam)

@subsection(@@subtitle)

A subtitle line under the title (also feeds titleslide).

@subtitle(Spring 2026)

@subsection(@@titlepage)

A cover page: title, author, date, then a page break.

@titlepage(title="Final Exam", author="Prof. X", date="2026")

@subsection(@@doc)

Document-wide settings: paper template, theme, shuffling, question numbering, default fill-in width.

@doc(template=ieee)

@doc(theme=dark, qnumbers)

@doc(shuffle=mc, fillsize=12)

@section(Headings)

@subsection(@@section)

A numbered section heading (h2).

@section(Part 1)

@section(Part 1, label=intro)

@section(Aside, number=0)

@subsection(@@subsection)

A numbered sub-heading (h3).

@subsection(Topic)

@subsection(Aside, number=0)

@subsection(@@subsubsection)

A numbered sub-sub-heading (h4).

@subsubsection(Detail)

@subsection(@@heading)

An UNNUMBERED heading (h2) — a section head that stays out of the numbering and the table of contents.

@heading(Agenda)

@subsection(@@h1)

Outline level 1 — the same heading as section, under its HTML/Word name.

@h1(Introduction)

@subsection(@@h2)

Outline level 2 — the same heading as subsection.

@h2(Background)

@subsection(@@h3)

Outline level 3 — the same heading as subsubsection.

@h3(Details)

@subsection(@@h4)

Outline level 4 — an unnumbered run-in heading at body size.

@h4(Fine point)

@subsection(@@h5)

Outline level 5 — an unnumbered run-in heading.

@h5(Finer point)

@subsection(@@h6)

Outline level 6 — the deepest unnumbered run-in heading.

@h6(Finest point)

@section(Contents and appendices)

@subsection(@@toc)

A table of contents.

@toc(figures)

@toc(tables)

@toc

@toc(sections)

@toc(cols=2)

@toc(sections, cols=auto)

@subsection(@@appendix)

Everything after this is an appendix: sections letter A, B, C and their subsections number A.1, A.2 — in the preview and the PDF alike.

@appendix

@@appendices is the same as @@appendix:
@appendices
@section(A lettered appendix)
This section is lettered.
